===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=childbearing|inh|enm>birthe>childbearing|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|birthe}} (1250), from earlier {{m|enm|burthe}}, {{m|enm|burde}},<ref>Robert K. Barnhart, ed., ''Chambers Dictionary of Etymology'' (1988; reprint, Edinburgh: Chambers, 2008), 95.</ref> from {{der|en|non|burðr}}, {{m|non|byrd}}<ref>Richard Cleasby and Gudbrand Vigfusson's '''1874''' Icelandic-English dictionary.</ref> ({{cog|gmq-osw|byrth}}, {{cog|sv|börd}}), replacing {{cog|ang|ġebyrd}} (rare variant {{m|ang|byrþ}}),<ref>Joseph Bosworth and T. Northcote Toller's '''1898''' Anglo-Saxon dictionary.</ref> equivalent to {{affix|en|bear|-th|id2=abstract nominal}} (thus a {{piecewise doublet|en|berth|nocap=1}}). The Old Norse is from {{der|en|gem-pro|*burdiz}} (compare {{cog|ofs|berde}}, {{m|ofs|berd}}); {{cog|ang|ġebyrd}} is from prefixed {{m|gem-pro|*gaburþiz}} (compare {{cog|nl|geboorte}}, {{cog|de|Geburt}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰértis|*bʰŕ̥tis}} (compare {{cog|la|fors||luck}}, {{cog|sga|brith}}), from {{m|ine-pro|*bʰer-||to carry, bear}}. More at {{l|en|bear}}.

====Noun====
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The process of [[childbearing]]; the beginning of [[life]]; the emergence of a human [[baby]] or other [[viviparous]] animal offspring from the mother's body into the environment.
# {{lb|en|countable}} An instance of [[childbirth]].
#: {{ux|en|Intersex babies account for roughly one per cent of all '''births'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A beginning or start; a point of origin.
#: {{co|en|the '''birth''' of an empire}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The circumstances of one's background, ancestry, or upbringing.
#: {{ux|en|He was of noble '''birth''', but fortune had not favored him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843|author=w:William H. Prescott|title=History Of The Conquest Of Mexico And History Of The Conquest Of Peru|url=https://archive.org/details/historyoftheconq027127mbp|page=42|text=without reference to '''birth''', but solely for their qualifications|publisher=The Modern Library}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1861|author=Anthony Trollope|title=Framley Parsonage
|passage=Lucy {{...}} had no fortune, which, though a minor evil, was an evil; and she had no '''birth''', in the high-life sense of the word, which was a greater evil.}}
# That which is born.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1692|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=The Works of Ben Jonson|url=http://hollowaypages.com/Jonson.htm|page=288|text=That poets are far rarer '''births''' than kings.|chapter=Epigrams|chapterurl=http://hollowaypages.com/jonson1692epigrams.htm}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1761|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=The Works of Joseph Addison|url=https://archive.org/details/worksoflateright03addi|page=49|text=Others hatch their eggs and tend the '''birth''' till it is able to shift for itself.|volume=3|publisher=w:John Baskerville}}

=====Usage notes=====
'''Birth''' and '''childbirth''': '''Childbirth''' connotes the event as it occurs to the mother, whereas '''birth''' connotes it as it occurs to the offspring. For example, "the pain of childbirth" suggests pain the mother feels, while "the pain of birth" suggests pain the baby feels. Either term can be used from an outside perspective (''Fathers are more and more frequently present '''at the birth/at childbirth''''').

